{"source_path":"/home/jeremy/.claude/projects/-srv-git-odbus-verified/f4c461eb-d25f-4247-9bd7-eddda9ade635.jsonl","source_line":88,"timestamp":"2026-10-19T01:46:30.537Z","uuid":"59e03650-6314-46d7-bc95-3e223d62afc6","parentUuid":"515ca94a-8429-4cd3-956b-0bc5e0a45cd8","sessionId":"f4c461eb-d25f-4247-9bd7-eddda9ade635","type":"user","role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Qq3X53p6Gcxg1atU9MFsG3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: btrfs\n3\tdescription: >\n4\t  Btrfs filesystem administration: subvolumes, snapshots, rollback, balance,\n5\t  scrub, RAID profiles, compression, quotas, send/receive backups, and\n6\t  troubleshooting.\n7\t  MUST consult when installing, configuring, or troubleshooting btrfs.\n8\ttriggerPhrases:\n9\t  - \"btrfs\"\n10\t  - \"Btrfs\"\n11\t  - \"btrfs snapshot\"\n12\t  - \"btrfs subvolume\"\n13\t  - \"btrfs balance\"\n14\t  - \"btrfs scrub\"\n15\t  - \"copy-on-write filesystem\"\n16\t  - \"CoW filesystem\"\n17\t  - \"btrfs-progs\"\n18\t  - \"mkfs.btrfs\"\n19\t  - \"snapper\"\n20\t  - \"btrbk\"\n21\tglobs:\n22\t  - \"**/fstab\"\n23\t  - \"**/etc/fstab\"\n24\tlast_verified: \"unverified\"\n25\t---\n26\t\n27\t## Identity\n28\t\n29\t| Property | Value |\n30\t|----------|-------|\n31\t| **Type** | Kernel built-in filesystem (no loadable module needed on most distros) |\n32\t| **CLI tool** | `btrfs` (subcommands: `filesystem`, `subvolume`, `balance`, `scrub`, `device`, `quota`, `qgroup`, `check`, `defragment`, `rescue`) |\n33\t| **Distro install** | `apt install btrfs-progs` / `dnf install btrfs-progs` |\n34\t| **Create** | `mkfs.btrfs` |\n35\t| **Kernel version matters** | Significant stability improvements landed between 4.x and 6.x; older kernels have known bugs with RAID-5/6 and balance |\n36\t\n37\t## Quick Start\n38\t\n39\t```bash\n40\tsudo apt install btrfs-progs\n41\tsudo mkfs.btrfs -L mydata /dev/sdX1\n42\tsudo mount /dev/sdX1 /mnt/mydata\n43\tbtrfs filesystem show /mnt/mydata\n44\tbtrfs device stats /mnt/mydata\n45\t```\n46\t\n47\t## Key Operations\n48\t\n49\t| Task | Command |\n50\t|------|---------|\n51\t| Show filesystems | `btrfs filesystem show` |\n52\t| Filesystem usage (detailed) | `btrfs filesystem usage /mount` |\n53\t| List subvolumes | `btrfs subvolume list /mount` |\n54\t| Create subvolume | `btrfs subvolume create /mount/name` |\n55\t| Delete subvolume | `btrfs subvolume delete /mount/name` |\n56\t| Create read-write snapshot | `btrfs subvolume snapshot /mount/src /mount/dst` |\n57\t| Create read-only snapshot | `btrfs subvolume snapshot -r /mount/src /mount/dst` |\n58\t| Rollback: delete current, rename snapshot | `btrfs subvolume delete /mount/@; mv /mount/@snapshot /mount/@` |\n59\t| Rollback: recreate subvolume from snapshot | `btrfs subvolume snapshot /mount/@snapshot /mount/@` |\n60\t| Balance start (all chunks) | `btrfs balance start /mount` |\n61\t| Balance start (metadata only) | `btrfs balance start -mconvert=raid1 /mount` |\n62\t| Balance status | `btrfs balance status /mount` |\n63\t| Balance cancel | `btrfs balance cancel /mount` |\n64\t| Scrub start | `btrfs scrub start /mount` |\n65\t| Scrub status | `btrfs scrub status /mount` |\n66\t| Scrub cancel | `btrfs scrub cancel /mount` |\n67\t| Check filesystem (read-only) | `btrfs check /dev/sdX` (unmounted) |\n68\t| Defragment file or directory | `btrfs filesystem defragment -r /mount` |\n69\t| Resize (grow) | `btrfs filesystem resize max /mount` |\n70\t| Resize (shrink) | `btrfs filesystem resize -10G /mount` |\n71\t| Add device | `btrfs device add /dev/sdY /mount` |\n72\t| Remove device | `btrfs device remove /dev/sdY /mount` |\n73\t| Device read/write error stats | `btrfs device stats /mount` |\n74\t| Enable quotas | `btrfs quota enable /mount` |\n75\t| Show qgroup usage | `btrfs qgroup show -reF /mount` |\n76\t\n77\t## Expected State\n78\t\n79\t- `btrfs device stats /mount` → all counters are `0` (any non-zero value is a read/write error)\n80\t- `btrfs scrub status /mount` → `Status: finished` with `0 errors`\n81\t- `btrfs balance status /mount` → `No balance found on '/mount'` when idle\n82\t- `btrfs filesystem usage /mount` → Data and metadata ratios show reasonable allocation (metadata should not be >80% full)\n83\t\n84\t## Health Checks\n85\t\n86\t1. `btrfs device stats /mount` → all counters `0`; any non-zero value means hardware errors, act immediately\n87\t2. `btrfs filesystem usage /mount` → check both Data and Metadata sections; metadata ENOSPC is the most common silent failure\n88\t3. `btrfs scrub status /mount` → verify last scrub completed with no errors; schedule scrubs monthly via systemd timer or cron\n89\t4. `btrfs subvolume list /mount` → audit snapshot count; accumulation fills disk without appearing in normal `df` output\n90\t\n91\t## Common Failures\n92\t\n93\t| Symptom | Cause | Fix |\n94\t|---------|-------|-----|\n95\t| `no space left on device` but `df` shows free space | Metadata chunks exhausted while data chunks exist | `btrfs filesystem usage /mount` — run `btrfs balance start -dusage=50 /mount` to rebalance underused chunks |\n96\t| RAID-1 on data but single on metadata | Dangerous default when adding a second disk; metadata is not redundant | `btrfs filesystem usage /mount` — check metadata profile; run `btrfs balance start -mconvert=raid1 /mount` |\n97\t| Filesystem mounts read-only | Encountered an error it cannot self-correct | Check `dmesg | grep -i btrfs`; run `btrfs check /dev/sdX` on unmounted device to assess damage |\n98\t| Balance fails with ENOSPC mid-run | Not enough free space to relocate all chunks | Use `-dusage=<N>` filter to relocate only partially-used chunks first; free up space before retrying |\n99\t| Qgroup show reports inflated usage | Qgroups track snapshot references — shared data counted in each | Shared data is expected; use `btrfs qgroup show -reF` to see exclusive vs shared bytes |\n100\t| Snapshot accumulation fills disk | Old snapshots reference deleted data, preventing space reclaim | `btrfs subvolume list /mount | grep snapshot` — delete old snapshots with `btrfs subvolume delete` |\n101\t| `btrfs check --repair` made it worse | `--repair` is not safe for general use and can corrupt data | Never run `--repair` without consulting upstream; use `rescue` subcommands instead |\n102\t| Filesystem errors after kernel upgrade | Older on-disk format or regression | Check `dmesg`; downgrade kernel or use `btrfs rescue` tools; check known issues for your kernel version |\n103\t\n104\t## Pain Points\n105\t\n106\t- **ENOSPC due to metadata/data imbalance**: The most common Btrfs \"gotcha\". Btrfs allocates space in chunks — if metadata chunks fill up, writes fail even when data chunks are available and `df` still shows free space. Fix: `btrfs balance start -dusage=50 /mount` to reclaim partially-empty chunks. This is not a bug — it is the expected behavior when chunk allocation becomes imbalanced.\n107\t- **Mixed RAID profiles between data and metadata**: Adding a second disk does not automatically mirror metadata. A system can have `RAID1` data but `single` metadata, meaning metadata loss on one disk is fatal. Always explicitly set both with `-d` and `-m` flags.\n108\t- **Snapshots are instant but they accumulate**: Snapshot creation is O(1) and free. Deletion requires reference counting and can be slow. Unreferenced snapshots hold on to deleted data indefinitely — disk usage only decreases after snapshot deletion completes.\n109\t- **`btrfs check` is slow and `--repair` is risky**: Unlike `fsck.ext4`, Btrfs check does not have a safe repair mode. `--repair` can corrupt a filesystem that `check` reports as damaged. Prefer `btrfs rescue` subcommands (`zero-log`, `fix-device-size`) for targeted fixes.\n110\t- **Btrfs RAID-5/6 has known parity issues**: As of kernels through 6.x, Btrfs RAID-5 and RAID-6 have unresolved write-hole bugs. Do not use RAID-5/6 for data you care about. Use RAID-1 or RAID-10 instead.\n111\t- **Send/receive for backups**: `btrfs send` / `btrfs receive` is efficient for incremental backups using read-only snapshots as base references. Tools like `snapper` and `btrbk` automate the snapshot lifecycle and send/receive workflow — prefer them over manual scripting.\n112\t- **CoW fragmentation on databases and VMs**: Copy-on-write writes new data to new locations, which fragments sequential files over time. Use `nodatacow` mount option (or `chattr +C`) for VM disk images, database files, and other large randomly-written files. `nodatacow` also disables checksums for those files.\n113\t\n114\t## See Also\n115\t\n116\t- **ext4** — Traditional Linux filesystem without CoW overhead; use when you need maximum compatibility and don't need snapshots\n117\t- **xfs** — High-performance filesystem for large sequential I/O; use for databases and media workloads where CoW fragmentation is a concern\n118\t- **zfs** — Full storage stack with pooling and replication; use when you need enterprise-grade checksums and send/receive without kernel version sensitivity\n119\t- **lvm** — Logical volume manager; provides snapshot and resize capabilities at the block layer beneath btrfs\n120\t\n121\t## References\n122\t\n123\tSee `references/` for:\n124\t- `btrfs-options.md` — mkfs and mount options organized by category\n125\t- `common-patterns.md` — filesystem setup, subvolume layouts, snapshots, rollback, backup, RAID, and compression\n126\t- `docs.md` — official documentation and community links\n127\t"}]}
