{"source_path":"/home/jeremy/.claude/projects/-srv-git-odbus-verified/f4c461eb-d25f-4247-9bd7-eddda9ade635.jsonl","source_line":90,"timestamp":"2026-10-19T01:46:31.028Z","uuid":"98b00fc9-aa94-44ed-b36d-fa81a5d61af0","parentUuid":"8276a0e0-dfbd-4c30-9b61-dc380e3d10d9","sessionId":"f4c461eb-d25f-4247-9bd7-eddda9ade635","type":"user","role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ELxDZFdC6xJKyLDAWhMDM2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: rust-memory-optimization\n3\tdescription: |\n4\t  Memory optimization and allocation reduction in Rust. Use when:\n5\t  - User is designing memory-sensitive or low-latency systems\n6\t  - Optimizing data structures to reduce memory footprint\n7\t  - Replacing heap allocations with stack-allocated/bounded structures (SmallVec, ArrayVec, ThinVec)\n8\t  - Handling small strings with optimizations (CompactString)\n9\t  - Reusing collections via clear() in loops\n10\t  - Implementing zero-copy decoding and references\n11\t  - Structuring arena-allocated memory (Bumpalo)\n12\t  - Measuring memory usage, profiling heap, or diagnosing leaks\n13\t---\n14\t\n15\t\n16\t\n17\t## Quick Navigation\n18\t\n19\t- [references/stack_allocations.md](references/stack_allocations.md)\n20\t- [references/arenas.md](references/arenas.md)\n21\t\n22\t# Rust Memory Optimization Guide\n23\t\n24\tAdvanced strategies for reducing allocations, shrinking layout sizes, and boosting performance.\n25\t\n26\t## Core Rules & Patterns\n27\t\n28\t### 1. Bounded Heap-Free Collections\n29\tFor collections that are usually small, avoid standard vectors to bypass heap allocations.\n30\t- **`SmallVec`**: Stack-allocated up to a threshold, overflows to the heap.\n31\t- **`ArrayVec`**: Fixed-capacity array wrapper; never allocates on the heap.\n32\t- **`ThinVec`**: Only takes a single pointer size when empty, reducing size in enum layouts.\n33\t\n34\t```rust\n35\tuse smallvec::{smallvec, SmallVec};\n36\tuse arrayvec::ArrayVec;\n37\t\n38\t// SmallVec: Allocates 4 items on the stack. Goes to heap only if count > 4.\n39\tlet mut local_tags: SmallVec<[String; 4]> = smallvec![];\n40\tlocal_tags.push(\"Rust\".to_string());\n41\t\n42\t// ArrayVec: Absolute limit of 8 items on the stack. No heap overflow.\n43\tlet mut coordinates: ArrayVec<f64, 8> = ArrayVec::new();\n44\tcoordinates.push(42.0);\n45\t```\n46\t\n47\t### 2. Small String Optimization (SSO)\n48\tStandard `String` consumes 24 bytes and allocates heap space immediately. Use `CompactString` for inline small strings (under 24 bytes) without allocating.\n49\t\n50\t```rust\n51\tuse compact_str::CompactString;\n52\t\n53\t// No heap allocation for strings <= 24 characters\n54\tlet username = CompactString::new(\"user_123\");\n55\t```\n56\t\n57\t### 3. Boxed Slices vs Vectors\n58\tIf a collection's size is determined once at initialization and never changes, convert it from `Vec<T>` to `Box<[T]>` to save 8 bytes of stack capacity space.\n59\t\n60\t```rust\n61\tlet items_vec: Vec<i32> = vec![1, 2, 3];\n62\t\n63\t// Save capacity field (shrinks stack representation from 24 to 16 bytes)\n64\tlet items_slice: Box<[i32]> = items_vec.into_boxed_slice();\n65\t```\n66\t\n67\t### 4. Zero-Copy Patterns\n68\tInstead of cloning or allocating strings, hold references to existing memory using slices or crates like `bytes::Bytes`.\n69\t\n70\t```rust\n71\tuse bytes::Bytes;\n72\t\n73\tpub struct Message {\n74\t    // Zero-copy reference-counted byte buffer slice\n75\t    pub payload: Bytes,\n76\t}\n77\t\n78\t// Can be sliced and shared among threads without cloning underlying memory\n79\tlet original = Bytes::from(vec![0; 1024]);\n80\tlet slice = original.slice(0..100); \n81\t```\n82\t\n83\t### 5. Arena Allocation\n84\tFor batch allocation where structures share a lifecycle (e.g. AST parsing), use arena allocators (`bumpalo`) to allocate items sequentially in a continuous region of memory and free them all at once.\n85\t\n86\t```rust\n87\tuse bumpalo::Bump;\n88\t\n89\tlet bump = Bump::new();\n90\t\n91\t// Allocates quickly on the bump arena\n92\tlet string_ref = bump.alloc_str(\"dynamic string\");\n93\tlet int_ref = bump.alloc(42);\n94\t\n95\t// Entire arena is cleared at once on drop\n96\t```\n97\t\n98\t### 6. Collection Reuse\n99\tReuse collection allocations in performance-critical loops with `.clear()` instead of re-instantiating.\n100\t\n101\t```rust\n102\t// Bad: Allocates a new vector every iteration\n103\tfor item in feed {\n104\t    let mut batch = Vec::new();\n105\t    batch.push(item);\n106\t}\n107\t\n108\t// Good: Reuses allocation buffer\n109\tlet mut batch = Vec::with_capacity(100);\n110\tfor item in feed {\n111\t    batch.clear();\n112\t    batch.push(item);\n113\t}\n114\t```\n115\t\n116\t## Layout Awareness\n117\t\n118\tUse `std::mem::size_of` to understand data layout.\n119\t\n120\t```rust\n121\tprintln!(\"User size: {}\", std::mem::size_of::<User>());\n122\t```\n123\t\n124\tReorder fields to reduce padding only when the type is numerous or hot.\n125\t\n126\t```rust\n127\t// Often smaller: group large-alignment fields first\n128\tstruct Record {\n129\t    id: u64,\n130\t    flags: u32,\n131\t    kind: u8,\n132\t}\n133\t```\n134\t\n135\t## String Strategy\n136\t\n137\t```rust\n138\tfn label<'a>(name: &'a str, fallback: &'a str) -> std::borrow::Cow<'a, str> {\n139\t    if name.is_empty() { fallback.into() } else { name.into() }\n140\t}\n141\t```\n142\t\n143\tAvoid `format!` in tight loops. Reuse buffers with `clear()`.\n144\t\n145\t## Zero-Copy Parsing\n146\t\n147\tReturn slices into the input when the input outlives parsed data.\n148\t\n149\t```rust\n150\tpub struct Header<'a> {\n151\t    pub name: &'a str,\n152\t    pub value: &'a str,\n153\t}\n154\t```\n155\t\n156\tDo not force zero-copy when it creates unmanageable lifetimes for little gain.\n157\t\n158\t## ThinVec for Enum Payloads\n159\t\n160\tWhen enums contain `Vec` variants, `ThinVec` reduces the enum size by storing only a pointer when empty:\n161\t\n162\t```rust\n163\tuse thin_vec::ThinVec;\n164\t\n165\t// Regular Vec in an enum makes the enum 24+ bytes even when empty\n166\tenum Node {\n167\t    Leaf(i64),\n168\t    Children(Vec<Node>),     // 24 bytes for Vec\n169\t}\n170\t\n171\t// ThinVec only stores 8 bytes (a pointer) when empty\n172\tenum NodeOptimized {\n173\t    Leaf(i64),\n174\t    Children(ThinVec<Node>), // 8 bytes for ThinVec\n175\t}\n176\t```\n177\t\n178\t## Cow for Conditional Ownership\n179\t\n180\t```rust\n181\tuse std::borrow::Cow;\n182\t\n183\tpub struct Error {\n184\t    message: Cow<'static, str>,\n185\t}\n186\t\n187\timpl Error {\n188\t    // No allocation for static messages\n189\t    pub fn new(msg: &'static str) -> Self {\n190\t        Self { message: Cow::Borrowed(msg) }\n191\t    }\n192\t\n193\t    // Allocates only when dynamic\n194\t    pub fn with_detail(msg: String) -> Self {\n195\t        Self { message: Cow::Owned(msg) }\n196\t    }\n197\t}\n198\t```\n199\t\n200\t## Arc vs Rc Trade-offs\n201\t\n202\t```rust\n203\tuse std::rc::Rc;\n204\tuse std::sync::Arc;\n205\t\n206\t// Single-threaded shared ownership — cheaper than Arc\n207\tlet shared = Rc::new(large_data);\n208\t\n209\t// Multi-threaded shared ownership — required across threads\n210\tlet shared = Arc::new(large_data);\n211\t```\n212\t\n213\tPrefer `Rc` when data never crosses thread boundaries. `Arc` adds atomic reference counting overhead.\n214\t\n215\t## HashMap Memory Optimization\n216\t\n217\t```rust\n218\tuse hashbrown::HashMap;\n219\t\n220\t// hashbrown's HashMap uses less memory than std's and is faster\n221\tlet mut map: HashMap<String, i32> = HashMap::with_capacity(128);\n222\t\n223\t// Reserve capacity upfront to avoid rehashing\n224\tmap.reserve(expected_count);\n225\t\n226\t// Consider FxHashMap for non-adversarial keys\n227\tuse rustc_hash::FxHashMap;\n228\tlet mut fast_map: FxHashMap<String, i32> = FxHashMap::default();\n229\t```\n230\t\n231\t## Stack vs Heap Decision Matrix\n232\t\n233\t| Type | Stack Size | Heap Alloc | Use When |\n234\t|------|-----------|------------|----------|\n235\t| `String` | 24 bytes | Yes | Owns mutable text |\n236\t| `&str` | 16 bytes | No | Borrowed text, lifetime bound |\n237\t| `CompactString` | 24 bytes | No (≤24 chars) | Short strings that own data |\n238\t| `Cow<str>` | 32 bytes | Conditionally | Mostly static, rarely dynamic |\n239\t| `Box<str>` | 16 bytes | Yes | Owned fixed string |\n240\t| `Vec<T>` | 24 bytes | Yes | Growable collection |\n241\t| `SmallVec<[T; N]>` | ~24+ bytes | Only if >N | Usually small, occasionally large |\n242\t| `Box<[T]>` | 16 bytes | Yes | Fixed-size after construction |\n243\t| `ThinVec<T>` | 8 bytes | Only if non-empty | Empty or small in enums |\n244\t\n245\t## Measuring Memory\n246\t\n247\t```rust\n248\tuse std::alloc::{GlobalAlloc, System, Layout};\n249\t\n250\tstruct TrackingAllocator;\n251\t\n252\tunsafe impl GlobalAlloc for TrackingAllocator {\n253\t    unsafe fn alloc(&self, layout: Layout) -> *mut u8 {\n254\t        System.alloc(layout)\n255\t    }\n256\t\n257\t    unsafe fn dealloc(&self, ptr: *mut u8, layout: Layout) {\n258\t        System.dealloc(ptr, layout)\n259\t    }\n260\t}\n261\t\n262\t// For detailed profiling, use dhat, heaptrack, or valgrind\n263\t```\n264\t\n265\tUse `dhat` crate for heap profiling in tests:\n266\t\n267\t```rust\n268\t#[cfg(test)]\n269\tmod tests {\n270\t    #[test]\n271\t    fn memory_test() {\n272\t        let _profiler = dhat::Profiler::new_heap();\n273\t        // code under test\n274\t        // dhat prints allocation stats on drop\n275\t    }\n276\t}\n277\t```\n278\t\n279\t## Optimization Workflow\n280\t\n281\t1. Measure allocations and CPU first.\n282\t2. Identify the hot path and expected data sizes.\n283\t3. Pick the narrowest memory change.\n284\t4. Benchmark before/after.\n285\t5. Keep readability unless the win is meaningful.\n286\t\n287\t## Allocation Decision Rules\n288\t\n289\t- `Vec<T>`: general purpose growable collection.\n290\t- `SmallVec<[T; N]>`: usually-small collection with occasional heap spill.\n291\t- `ArrayVec<T, N>`: hard maximum size, no heap allocation.\n292\t- `Box<[T]>`: fixed-size heap slice after construction.\n293\t- `Bytes`: shared immutable byte buffers.\n294\t- `Bump`: many same-lifetime allocations.\n295\t- `Cow<'a, T>`: borrow unless mutation/ownership is required.\n296\t\n297\t## Anti-Patterns\n298\t\n299\t```rust\n300\t// Bad: cloning a large Vec just to avoid lifetime complexity\n301\tlet data = original.clone(); // expensive and unnecessary\n302\t\n303\t// Good: use Arc or Cow for shared ownership\n304\tlet data: Arc<[u8]> = original.into();\n305\t```\n306\t\n307\t```rust\n308\t// Bad: allocating inside a hot loop\n309\tfor item in items {\n310\t    let s = format!(\"processing {}\", item); // allocates every iteration\n311\t}\n312\t\n313\t// Good: reuse a buffer\n314\tlet mut buf = String::with_capacity(128);\n315\tfor item in items {\n316\t    buf.clear();\n317\t    write!(buf, \"processing {}\", item).unwrap();\n318\t}\n319\t```\n320\t\n321\t- Replacing every `Vec` with `SmallVec` without measuring.\n322\t- Keeping arena references after reset.\n323\t- Cloning strings to satisfy ownership instead of shortening borrow scopes.\n324\t- Using `Arc` as a default clone workaround.\n325\t- Optimizing layout for types created a handful of times.\n326\t- Using `Box<dyn Trait>` when generics would eliminate the allocation entirely.\n327\t\n328\t## Review Prompt\n329\t\n330\tWhen reviewing memory optimization, ask what was measured, which allocation is removed, what lifetime assumptions changed, and whether the new type increases stack size or API complexity.\n331\t\n332\t## Optimization Checklist\n333\t\n334\t- Measure allocations before changing data structures.\n335\t- Preallocate collections only when capacity is known or bounded.\n336\t- Prefer borrowing and `Cow` before cloning strings or buffers.\n337\t- Use arenas only when lifetimes are truly batch-scoped.\n338\t- Check stack growth when replacing heap allocations with inline storage.\n339\t- Re-benchmark after changes and keep the simpler version if gains are noise.\n340\t- Profile with `dhat` or `heaptrack` to find actual allocation hotspots.\n341\t- Consider `hashbrown` or `rustc-hash` for HashMap-heavy code.\n342\t\n343\t## References\n344\t\n345\t- [Rust Performance Book: Heap Allocations](https://nnethercote.github.io/perf-book/heap-allocations.html)\n346\t- [smallvec](https://docs.rs/smallvec)\n347\t- [arrayvec](https://docs.rs/arrayvec)\n348\t- [bumpalo](https://docs.rs/bumpalo)\n349\t- [compact_str](https://docs.rs/compact_str)\n350\t- [bytes](https://docs.rs/bytes)\n351\t- [dhat](https://docs.rs/dhat)\n352\t- [hashbrown](https://docs.rs/hashbrown)\n353\t"}]}
